   "reasoning": "We need to analyze. Link record: route length 51.46 km, commissioning loss 12.12 dB at 1550, 19.93 at 1310. Design attenuator 0 dB. Alarm thresholds: RX_LOW_WARNING -13, RX_LOW_ALARM -16.\n\nPM: rx power stable -11.2 dBm until Oct 19, then drops to -21.2 dBm average. Oct 19 day min -21.35 max -11.05 avg -19.15 suggests transition around 13:00. That matches alarm raised 2026-10-19 13:00 and site notes: Receiver module at site B replaced under RMA. Attenuator refitted from spares box.\n\nPower measurements: A-TX 1.04 dBm (within spec -1 to +2). B-ODF -11.13 dBm before attenuator. So link loss A-TX to B-ODF = 1.04 - (-11.13) = 12.17 dB, which matches commissioning 12.12 dB. So fibre itself is fine.\n\nB-RX -21.28 dBm after attenuator. So difference B-ODF to B-RX = -11.13 - (-21.28) = 10.15 dB. That's attenuation of B-ATT. Design is 0 dB, so wrong attenuator inserted. Available values include 10 dB, which would be close to 10.15 dB measured (maybe 10 dB attenuator + 0.15 insertion loss). So root cause is wrong attenuator.\n\nCheck OTDR: Both wavelengths event tables show no anomalous loss. Losses all ~0.05-0.09, normal splices. Event at 35.887 km reflective loss 0.21 is MID-PP connector, normal. No macrobend signature (would be wavelength dependent higher loss at 1550 than 1310). No bad splice with high loss. No fibre break (would be LOS at -25). So fibre is fine.\n\nTx power daily avg stable ~1.17 dBm, bias stable ~36 mA, so Tx not degraded.\n\nSite notes: attenuator refitted from spares box on 2026-10-19 12:22, which aligns exactly with drop.\n\nThus fault_type = wrong_attenuator, element_id = B-ATT, action = replace_attenuator.\n\nNeed to call submit_diagnosis. Provide evidence: Commissioning loss 12.12 dB, measured A-TX 1.04 dBm, B-ODF -11.13 dBm gives link loss 12.17 dB normal, OTDR shows no excess loss, B-RX -21.28 dBm indicates ~10 dB excess attenuation between B-ODF and B-RX, coinciding with attenuator refit on Oct 19, causing RX_LOW_ALARM etc.\n\nAlso check tx degradation? No, tx power stable.\n\nCheck dirty connector? Could be but B-ODF power normal, and site notes cleaned B ODF Oct 17 passed inspection. Also OTDR reflective events normal reflectance -52 to -55 dB.\n\nSo diagnosis is wrong_attenuator.\n\nNeed to provide location: element_id B-ATT, route_km null? For wrong_attenuator, required element_id.\n\nAction replace_attenuator.\n\nEvidence should cite measurements.\n\nWe should also maybe double-check to ensure no other possibilities: Could be dirty/damaged connector at B-ATT? But B-ODF before attenuator is good, B-RX after attenuator is low by 10 dB. Could be dirty connector causing 10 dB loss, but notes says attenuator refitted from spares box - implies wrong value. How to distinguish dirty vs wrong attenuator? Measure power shows 10.15 dB loss exactly matches available attenuator 10 dB value. Dirty connector would be more variable, unlikely exactly 10 dB. Also we could inspect connector but element_id B-ATT is attenuator. The fault_type options: dirty_connector would be at A-ODF, MID-PP, B-ODF. But measurement shows B-ODF is before attenuator, so if B-ODF connector were dirty, B-ODF reading would be low, but it's not. So not dirty.\n\nAction for wrong_attenuator is replace_attenuator with design 0 dB.\n\nWe should call submit_diagnosis.\n\nWe also need to ensure we have checked other metrics: pre_fec_ber maybe? But already have evidence.\n\nProvide thorough evidence.\n\n",
